---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ConverterHero from '../components/ConverterHero.astro';
import FAQAccordion from '../components/FAQAccordion.astro';
import RelatedPairs from '../components/RelatedPairs.astro';

const site = Astro.site ?? new URL('https://usdtoworld.com');
const pairFaqs = [
  {
    q: 'Is EUR to USD the same rate as USD to EUR, just flipped?',
    a: "Yes — mathematically, the EUR/USD rate and USD/EUR rate are exact reciprocals of each other, always. If 1 EUR buys 1.08 USD, then 1 USD buys roughly 0.93 EUR. This page just lets you start from euros instead of dollars, which is more natural if you're a eurozone resident figuring out what your euros are worth abroad.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does the euro sometimes get called a "basket" currency?',
    a: "That's not a standard technical term for the euro, but people sometimes describe it that way informally because it represents a shared currency across many different national economies (Germany, France, Italy, Spain, and 16+ others) with varying growth rates, debt levels, and inflation. This makes the euro's overall value a kind of blended reflection of the eurozone as a whole, which can behave differently than any single member country's economy would suggest on its own.",
  },
  {
    q: 'Do eurozone countries all use exactly the same monetary policy?',
    a: "Yes — the European Central Bank sets one interest rate policy that applies across the entire eurozone, regardless of whether an individual member country's economy is running hot or cool at any given time. This is a structural feature (and occasional point of tension) of the currency union, since a rate that suits Germany may not suit Greece or Italy at the same moment, and it's part of why eurozone monetary policy decisions can be more complex than in single-country economies.",
  },
];

const breadcrumbSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: site.toString() },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'EUR to USD', item: new URL('/eur-to-usd', site).toString() },
  ],
};
const faqSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: pairFaqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
};
---
<Layout
  title="EUR to USD Converter — Live Euro to Dollar Rate | UsdtoWorld"
  description="Convert Euros to US Dollars with a live exchange rate. Get 1 EUR to USD, 100 EUR to USD, and 1000 EUR to USD instantly, on the world's most-traded pair."
  path="/eur-to-usd"
  schema={[breadcrumbSchema, faqSchema]}
  speakableSelectors={['h1', '.hero-sub']}
>
  <div class="container page-hero">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'EUR to USD', href: '/eur-to-usd' }]} />
  </div>

  <ConverterHero
    title="EUR to USD Converter (Live Exchange Rate)"
    subtitle="Convert Euros to US Dollars instantly with a live market rate — for eurozone travelers, businesses invoicing in dollars, and anyone tracking the world's most-traded pair from the euro side."
    defaultFrom="EUR"
    defaultTo="USD"
  />

  <section class="section editorial">
    <div class="container editorial-body prose">
      <p class="editorial-byline">Reviewed by the <a href="/about#who-writes-and-reviews-this">UsdtoWorld editorial team</a> · Sourced from live market rate data</p>
      <h2>Same pair, other direction</h2>
      <p>
        EUR/USD is the world's most-traded currency pair. That's true from either side. You get deep
        liquidity, tight spreads, and prices that update in real time. This version of the page starts
        from euros. It's built for eurozone residents traveling to the US, businesses billing US clients,
        and anyone turning euro savings into dollars.
      </p>

      <h2>What moves this rate</h2>
      <p>
        The biggest driver is simple: interest rates. When the <a href="https://www.ecb.europa.eu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">European Central Bank</a> and the US
        Federal Reserve set rates, the gap between them tends to move the euro. A few other things matter too:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Rate gap:</strong> a narrowing gap between ECB and Fed rates tends to support the euro. A widening gap tends to support the dollar.</li>
        <li><strong>One policy, many countries:</strong> the ECB sets one rate for the whole eurozone. That rate is a compromise across very different economies, so euro moves can be harder to predict short-term.</li>
        <li><strong>Safe-haven demand:</strong> the dollar often gains during market stress, no matter what's happening in Europe or the US specifically.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>For eurozone businesses invoicing in dollars</h2>
      <p>
        If you bill US clients in dollars, the rate on the day you convert matters. A good rate on invoice
        date can turn bad by the time payment arrives, weeks later. Some businesses use forward contracts or
        multi-currency accounts to manage this risk. For smaller amounts, converting promptly — instead of
        holding dollars and waiting — usually limits how much the rate can hurt you.
      </p>

      <h2>The euro's rocky first decade</h2>
      <p>
        The euro didn't have a smooth start. After launching, it actually traded below its starting value for
        a few years before recovering and strengthening through the mid-2000s. Then, starting around 2010, the
        eurozone debt crisis hit. Markets grew worried about government debt in Greece, Ireland, Portugal,
        Spain, and Italy. The ECB and eurozone governments responded with bailout programs and new rules to
        stabilize things. The euro survived, but the episode still shapes how markets judge eurozone risk today.
      </p>

      <h2>Common mistakes for eurozone businesses and travelers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Ignoring small dollar invoices:</strong> treating occasional dollar payments as a minor detail, then getting caught off guard when a larger contract's payment lands after a big rate move.</li>
        <li><strong>Trusting ATM rates abroad:</strong> assuming your card's withdrawal rate matches the mid-market rate on a converter. Card networks and ATM operators usually add their own margin or a flat fee — check your card issuer's terms before relying on ATMs while traveling.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>A note on eurozone economic diversity</h2>
      <p>
        "The eurozone economy" isn't really one thing. Germany's economy leans on exports. France leans more
        on domestic spending. Smaller member countries have their own patterns entirely. All of that gets
        blended into eurozone-wide data, like inflation reports. So when EUR/USD moves on a eurozone data
        release, remember: that number is an average across very different economies. That's part of why
        eurozone policy is harder to communicate — and harder to predict — than policy for a single country.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FAQAccordion
    faqs={pairFaqs}
    id="eur-usd-faq"
    eyebrow="EUR/USD FAQ"
    heading="Common EUR to USD questions"
    description="Quick answers before you convert."
  />

  <RelatedPairs
    links={[
      { label: 'USD to EUR', href: '/usd-to-eur' },
      { label: 'GBP to USD', href: '/gbp-to-usd' },
      { label: 'USD to GBP', href: '/usd-to-gbp' },
      { label: 'USD to CAD', href: '/usd-to-cad' },
    ]}
  />

  <script>
    import '../scripts/converter.js';
  </script>
</Layout>

<style>
  .editorial-body { max-width: 780px; }
</style>
